TITLE: The Sheriff Who Runs a Crowded Jail and Calls It Public Safety

SUMMARY: Kevin Turner ran in 2018 on integrity, accountability, and school safety. He won with backing from the local GOP establishment and has held the job ever since. What the campaign materials did not say: the jail he now commands holds more than 1,200 people on any given day in a facility designed for 900. Between 70 and 85 percent of those people have not been convicted of anything. Two people died in custody in a single weekend in February 2022. A third died in August 2025. The medical contractor running healthcare inside that jail is Wellpath — a company that has faced wrongful death litigation in jails across the country. Turner's campaign has collected over $322,000 since 2017, including $15,000 from attorney Mark McDaniel, $12,784 from Woody Anderson Ford, $10,000 from TSA PAC, and an estimated $15,000 aggregated from bondsmen and bail industry donors. The Sheriff commands more than half of Madison County's general fund. He has never faced a serious accountability review.

DETAILS: Kevin Turner did not arrive at the Madison County Sheriff's Office from the outside. He was born into it — or close enough. His father, Howard Turner, spent 34 years as a Huntsville Police Department investigator before moving to the U.S. Marshals Service and then the Madison County District Attorney's Office. Law enforcement is the family business, and Kevin Turner entered it in 1992 as a patrol deputy at the Madison County Sheriff's Office (MCSO).

He did not stay on patrol long. After certification through the Alabama Peace Officers' Standards and Training Commission (APOSTC) in Jacksonville, he moved into juvenile and adult investigations, then became North Alabama's first certified gang investigator after completing training through the National Gang Crime Research Center in Chicago — earning the Frederick Milton Thrasher Award in 2004. He was assigned to the North East Alabama Regional Drug Task Force as a narcotics agent and gang investigator, then to the Madison County Drug Task Force in the same dual role. In September 2010, he was appointed Chief Investigator for the Madison County District Attorney's Office, commanding the Criminal Investigations Division, Worthless Check Unit, Restitution and Recovery unit, Computer Forensics Unit, and Violent Crime Unit.

He ran for Sheriff in 2018 after longtime Sheriff Blake Dorning retired. He won with 56 percent of the vote over Democrat Tim Clardy. His platform: integrity, accountability, school safety. His backers: the local Republican establishment and a donor list heavy with real estate, construction, bondsmen, and business PACs. He was re-elected in 2022. His current term expires December 31, 2026.

What Turner runs is not a small operation. The Madison County Sheriff commands over 400 employees, oversees an average daily jail population of more than 1,200 inmates, and controls a budget that consumed more than half of the county's general fund in 2025 — a budget that has grown from roughly $54 million in FY2023 to over $60 million in FY2024, an 11 percent single-year increase. The Huntsville-Madison County Jail is a joint venture between Madison County, the City of Huntsville, and the Public Building Authority. Huntsville pays Madison County $2.1 million per year under a 2010 intergovernmental agreement for jail operating costs. The Joint Jail Fund collected $4.59 million in FY2024 and spent $21.48 million — a gap of nearly $17 million covered by county appropriations.

The jail runs on private contractors. Food service: Summit Food Service, approved through the Madison County Commission. Healthcare: Southern Health Partners. Inmate phone and remote visitation: NCIC, at $0.16 per minute for calls and $0.30 per minute for remote visits. Inmate care packs through Kimble's, with a $4.95 handling fee, an 11 percent surcharge, and a $75 weekly cap. Personal mail is digitized and the originals destroyed after seven days. Inmates must be incarcerated 30 days before they are permitted any visitation at all.

The jail is chronically overcrowded. It was designed for approximately 900 to 1,000 people. It routinely holds 1,100 to 1,200. Between 70 and 85 percent of the people inside have not been convicted of any crime. Turner's campaign has received an estimated $15,000 in aggregated contributions from bondsmen and bail industry donors.

Three people have died in that jail on Turner's watch with documented public records. On February 4, 2022, a 48-year-old woman died by suicide using a bedsheet. On February 6, 2022, a 28-year-old man was found unresponsive and died. On August 11, 2025, Demetrius Lemonte Stewart, 52, was found unresponsive during meal service after morning count. He had been booked hours earlier by Huntsville Police on a probation violation. He was pronounced dead. A complete list of in-custody deaths during Turner's tenure has not been publicly released.

Turner has also been named in civil litigation. April Grubb filed a civil lawsuit naming Turner and the City of Huntsville after she said she was struck six times by rubber bullets during a June 3, 2020 protest. In Chandler Korb et al. v. Victor Flores De Leon Jr. et al., federal civil rights claims naming Turner and Madison County were ultimately dismissed.

