DECODER — THE RISE (gold):
Turner built his entire career inside one institution: Madison County law enforcement. His father was a 34-year Huntsville Police investigator. Kevin started as a patrol deputy at MCSO in 1992 and never left the county's orbit. He accumulated certifications — gang investigator, narcotics agent, chief investigator — rising through the Madison County District Attorney's Office over 20 years until he commanded every investigative unit the DA ran. When the Sheriff retired in 2018, Turner stepped into the race with full establishment backing. He won with 56 percent. He has held the office since, expanding a budget that now consumes more than half of the county's general fund and commands over 400 employees. He has never faced a meaningful accountability review.

DECODER — THE AFFILIATIONS (blue):
Alabama Republican Executive Committee — $6,919.65 in campaign contributions. The party apparatus moved money directly into his campaign.
Madison County Republican Executive Committee — $10,699.50 in campaign expenditures; Turner routed a significant share of his spending through the county party infrastructure.
TSA PAC — $10,000 donor. Organizational identity and interests not publicly disclosed.
Alabama Realtors PAC — $6,000 donor. The real estate industry's political arm funded the Sheriff's campaign. Turner's office enforces evictions and property-related civil process.
FPII PAC — $4,900 donor. Organizational identity and interests not publicly disclosed.
North Alabama PAC — $4,500 donor. Regional political action committee aligned with North Alabama Republican business interests.
Bondsmen and bail industry donors — estimated $15,000 aggregated. The industry that profits from the pretrial detention system Turner administers contributed to his campaigns.
Politically Correct Consulting — $48,049.88 in campaign expenditures, the single largest vendor payment from Turner's committee.
Wellpath (formerly Correct Care Solutions) — jail healthcare contractor. Wellpath has faced wrongful death litigation in detention facilities across the United States.
National Gang Crime Research Center — trained Turner, certified him, gave him the Thrasher Award. His entire professional identity runs through this institutional network.

DECODER — THE BENEFICIARIES (lavender):
Mark McDaniel — $15,000 in campaign contributions to Kevin Turner. Attorney operating in Madison County courts that Turner's office feeds cases into.
Woody Anderson Ford — $12,784 in campaign contributions. Vehicle dealership; the Sheriff's Office maintains a fleet requiring regular procurement and service contracts.
Angel Rey Almodovar — $6,000 in campaign contributions. Identity and interests not publicly disclosed.
The Broadway Group LLC — $6,000 in campaign contributions. Identity and interests not publicly disclosed.
Trident Security Solutions LLC — $5,000 in campaign contributions. Security firm contributing to the campaign of the county's top law enforcement officer.
Jeffrey Enfinger — $5,000 in campaign contributions. Identity and interests not publicly disclosed.
Bobby White — $5,000 in campaign contributions. Identity and interests not publicly disclosed.
Summit Food Service — jail food contractor. Contract amount not publicly disclosed.
Southern Health Partners — jail healthcare contractor. Contract amount not publicly disclosed.
NCIC — jail phone and remote visitation contractor. Charges inmates $0.16/minute for calls, $0.30/minute for remote visits.

DECODER — THE TRACK RECORD (red):
— Feb. 4, 2022: 48-year-old woman died by suicide in Madison County jail. Cause: bedsheet.
— Feb. 6, 2022: 28-year-old man died in custody, medical episode, two days after the first death.
— Aug. 11, 2025: Demetrius Lemonte Stewart, 52, found unresponsive during meal service hours after booking on a probation violation. Pronounced dead.
— Complete in-custody death list under Turner's tenure: not publicly released.
— Jail design capacity: est. 900–1,000. Routine daily population: 1,100–1,200. Overcrowded every day.
— 70–85% of jail population is pretrial — not convicted of any crime.
— June 3, 2020: April Grubb sued Turner and the City of Huntsville alleging she was struck by six rubber bullets during a protest response.
— Korb v. De Leon: federal civil rights case named Turner; claims against Turner and Madison County were dismissed.
— FY2024 public safety budget: $60,300,921 — an 11.28% single-year increase.
— 2025 budget: more than half of the county general fund goes to the Sheriff's Office and jail.
— Private jail contractors operate under contracts with amounts not publicly disclosed.

